ent Coordinator, helping potential surrogates get started on their own surrogacy journeys. I can’t imagine a job more fulfilling and I am still able to help grow families every day. Ashley Hellstern, ConceiveAbilities Surrogacy Educator and wife of retired Coast Guard Commander Ashley understands the needs of the military community on a personal level. Her experience is a great resource for military families as they explore their surrogacy options. Becoming a surrogate is a big family decision. Read more about Everything You Need to Know About Surrogacy to learn more about the surrogacy process and join the Surrogacy Learning Center to connect with experienced surrogates and surrogacy experts. Surrogate Sisterhood: Welcome to the Club! It takes a special kind of woman to become a surrogate. Our surrogates report feeling a sense of empowerment and purpose. ConceiveAbilities’ surrogate sisterhood provides women a chance to be a part of something larger than themselves. Support groups, online communities like our private facebook group and the Surrogacy Learning Center allows our surrogates to connect with other women like them who have shared experiences. Belonging to the military community is a special and unique experience. Military families show sacrifice and commitment every day. Similarly, surrogates are helping others achieve their American dream… a family. With these amazing surrogates, that dream can be achieved. Surrogacy is a calling to help those who cannot have a baby do so. Our surrogates report feeling a deep calling to become a surrogate and they have a life changing experience giving the gift of family. According to Blue Star Families, 77% of military spouse respondents report at least one circumstance of underemployment; their pay is lower than their education level, their pay is lower than their work experience, they are overqualified for their current position, their pay is lower than previous positions, or they worked fewer hours than they wanted. As a surrogate, you have the opportunity to earn a limited time bonus in addition to your base pay that increases based on location and experience. Find out your estimated pay with our surrogacy pay calculator. Personalized support every step of the surrogacy process Top surrogacy compensation Right timing based on your family needs Fourth trimester postpartum recovery care Monthly gifts Unlimited opportunity to earn referral rewards for your family While not the main driving factor, compensation is a deciding factor of becoming a surrogate. 81% have a financial desire to create flexibility and a safety net for her family. 74% of our surrogates report being influenced by being able to lessen family financial stressors like paying off loans, starting college funds and having a down payment for a family home as a reason to become a surrogate. Surrogate compensation can also provide financial flexibility as a family transitions into civilian life. Basic Surrogate Qualifications for Military Spouses 5 Basic requirements to be a surrogate: Have you given birth before? A history of at least one full-term, healthy and uncomplicated pregnancy is essential to ensuring your surrogacy journey is healthy and successful. How old are you? At the time you are matched with intended parents, you should be between 21 and 40 years of age. Are you a long-term US resident? The surrogacy journey is lengthy and is accomplished only by working with a US-based IVF clinic. A current or pending deployment may add some extra considerations to the timing of when you are a surrogate but, with our expertise, we will help you find a way forward to be a surrogate. Are you physically healthy? You must have an uncomplicated medical history, not be over- or under-weight, not currently smoke, and be free of certain chronic conditions. Are you emotionally healthy? Your surrogacy journey is demanding and requires a stable foundation in your life without undue stress. Do you qualify to be a surrogate?TRICARE Insurance and Surrogacy While TRICARE for surrogacy healthcare is a limited benefit, as experts in working with military families, ConceiveAbilities knows how to ensure you are fully covered to receive the very best medical care from the very best experts throughout your surrogacy journey. A ConceiveAbilities surrogate will have additional insurance, purchased and paid for as part of the All-In Care and Compensation Package, to cover all expenses of the surrogacy journey and considering every contingency scenario. Your job? Focus on a successful and empowering surrogacy experience, with peace of mind knowing that all the money matters are taken care of. Become a Surrogate",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/surrogates/surrogate-military/"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: Surrogacy Explained: Your Complete Guide to the Journey\n\nContent: Surrogacy with Conceiveabilities At ConceiveAbilities, we believe becoming a surrogate is one of the most courageous things someone can do. Start Your Surrogacy JourneyMany on our team have been surrogates or became parents through a gestational surrogate, shaping our commitment as a trusted surrogacy agency. We’ve built a process, leveraging our many years of experience, carefully designed to protect, support, and celebrate you throughout your surrogacy journey. If you’re curious, ready, or somewhere in between – we are your people. How to Become a Surrogate Becoming a surrogate with ConceiveAbilities involves five clear steps, each fully supported by our team of surrogacy experts. 1.Apply and ScreeningComplete our simple application process and complete our screening process to confirm you’re ready to become a surrogate.2.Get MatchedOur experienced surrogacy agency matches you with compatible intended parents, ensuring your comfort and alignment throughout your journey.3.Prepare for IVFOur experts guide you through the medication protocols and preparations for embryo transfer and pregnancy that are provided by your intended parents’ clinic.4.Carry the PregnancyReceive ongoing emotional, physical, and logistical support throughout your pregnancy from our dedicated match management team.5.Delivery and BeyondCelebrate this incredible milestone, with continuing support and recognition throughout your “fourth trimester” for your generous act as a surrogate.Learn About Becoming a SurrogateWho Qualifies to Be a Surrogate?Be between 21–42 years oldHave had at least one healthy pregnancy and deliveryBe a citizen or permanent resident of the United StatesWhat else? Complete the application and schedule an introductory call with one of our experts. We’re thrilled to know you and learn about your interest in surrogacy and, in that introductory call, we’ll tackle any other topics that could impact your qualifications to be a surrogate.Defining the Surrogate Mother What is a surrogate? Gestational surrogates, often called “surrogate mothers,” carry pregnancies for intended parents but have no genetic link to the child. While some people use “surrogate mother” casually, others prefer “surrogate” or “surrogate pregnancy” to better reflect the surrogate’s role. We use varied terminology to educate clearly and respectfully about this important family-building process. Understanding SurrogacySurrogate Support Program Our groundbreaking All-In Surrogate Care and Compensation Program provides unmatched support at every stage of your surrogacy journey. With our proprietary Matching Matters™ process, highest surrogate pay, comprehensive legal and medical coverage, and dedicated emotional support, we ensure you’re fully protected and empowered from application through postpartum recovery—the way surrogacy should be. Explore Surrogate SupportHow Are Surrogates Matched? Surrogates are matched with intended parents through our Matching Matters™ process based on shared values, expectations, and compatibility. We have dedicated staff just to understand your needs and expectations of who you see as your perfect match. You’ll have the opportunity to review the profile of any intended parent to decide whether you would like to meet them. And then, you’ll meet potential intended parents and choose who you feel most comfortable with. With a 97% first-time match success rate, we ensure meaningful relationships and smooth journeys from the start. See How Matching WorksWhat Medications Do Surrogates Take? You will work with your intended parents’ fertility clinic who will direct and guide your medication protocol to prepare your body for a successful transfer of the embryo and healthy pregnancy. These medications could include: birth control pills to regulate your cycle, Lupron to suppress ovulation, Estrogen to thicken your uterine lining, Progesterone and Medrol to support implantation, low-dose aspirin to improve blood flow, and antibiotics to prevent infection. While this may seem daunting, ConceiveAbilities manages a team of experts, with the fertility clinic at the center of this stage of your surrogacy journey, to provide guidance throughout the entire process, ensuring you’re comfortable and supported each step of the way. Learn More About Surrogacy MedicationsHow Much Do Surrogates Get Paid? First-time surrogates earn up to $75,000 – well above the national average – as participants in ConceiveAbilities’ All-In Care and Compensation program. Our simple payment structure ensures you’re financially supported every step of the way, with clear, upfront payments and no hidden requirements. Calculate Your Surrogate PaySurrogacy Laws By State Most states support compensated gestational surrogacy, with the exception of Nebraska and Louisiana. We offer detailed guidance on the legal landscape in every state to help you understand your rights and navigate the process with confidence. Check Your States Surrogacy LawsSurrogacy Terms Surrogacy is a complex journey with legal, medical, and psychological terms that can feel overwhelming at first. Our surrogacy glossary breaks it all down into clear, easy-to-understand language so you can know everything you need to throughout your surrogacy process. View the Surrogacy GlossaryWhat Surrogates Have to Say Others have stood where you are now—curious, hopeful, and ready to help. Hear firsthand from surrogates who partnered with ConceiveAbilities to make parenthood possible for families across the country. Hear From SurrogatesBeing a surrogate mom has been an incredibly fulfilling journey, and I couldn’t have asked for a better team to work with than Conceiveabilities. If you’re considering surrogacy, I wholeheartedly recommend this agency for their professionalism, empathy, and dedication. - Kelly, Chicago SurrogateI am a repeat surrogate with Conceiveabilities. I love working with this agency. They do such a wonderful job with their matching and their mental health team is fantastic! They’ve held my hand every step of the way and made me feel like I had a voice in how my journey progressed. - Christina, Colorado SurrogateWhat an amazing experience my Conceiveabilities journey was! They made sure communication was always happening and the structure behind the support system really empowered me. Being able to relax knowing everything had been talked about ahead of time, even things I hadn’t considered, really was a blessing. - Kimmee, Minnesota SurrogateOther Surrogacy Programs Looking to learn more about the different paths to surrogacy? Explore additional programs designed to meet unique needs and circumstances for both surrogates and intended parents. Refer a Surrogate Know someone who would make an amazing surrogate? Refer her to ConceiveAbilities and earn rewards while helping another family grow. Make a ReferralSurrogacy Advocate Program Our Surrogacy Advocate Program connects you with experienced surrogates who can answer your questions and share real insights. Get the support and perspective you need from someone who’s been there. Be a Surrogacy AdvocateSurrogacy FAQs More Surrogacy FAQsHow long does the surrogacy process take?The surrogate process may take between 15 to 18 months on average; however, any individual situation could take more or less time.If I am currently breastfeeding, can I apply to be a surrogate?Yes, should you meet the basic requirements, we will work with you in completing our qualification process. To start the surrogacy process itself, you must have completely weaned and have had at least one menstrual cycle.Can I apply to be a surrogate if I have an IUD in place for my birth control?Yes, you can apply to be a surrogate if you have an IUD and, assuming you meet the basic requirements, we will work through the entire qualification process with you. To start the surrogacy process itself, you must have had your IUD removed and have had at least one menstrual cycle.Does receiving government assistance disqualify me?No. A surrogate must demonstrate financial stability in order to qualify for the surrogacy program, however, receiving government assistance is not on its own a disqualifying factor.Ready to Begin Your Surrogacy Journey? You’ve learned what it means to be a surrogate—now it’s your turn to make a difference. When you partner with ConceiveAbilities, you’re supported, celebrated, and empowered from day one. Join the women who are helping build families and changing lives in the most meaningful way. Start Your ApplicationAdditional Resources Explore more topics and personal perspectives to support you wherever you are in your surrogacy journey. Surrogacy for Lactating MothersLearn how surrogates who choose to pump can provide continued care after delivery — and receive additional compensation for their time and effort.Surrogacy for Military WivesMilitary wives are uniquely positioned to be amazing surrogates. Discover how surrogacy can offer flexibility, purpose, and financial empowerment.Meet the Men Behind SurrogacySurrogacy isn’t just a women’s story — hear from the men who support, encourage, and stand proudly behind the journey.Lactation for New MothersUnderstand how lactation plays a role for intended mothers, and explore your options for feeding and bonding after surrogacy.Surrogacy in the Teaching CommunityMany teachers find surrogacy to be a natural extension of their nurturing spirit. Learn why educators make incredible surrogates.Surrogacy in the Nursing CommunityNurses bring compassion, expertise, and strength to surrogacy. See how their caregiving backgrounds make them ideal candidates for this life-changing journey.",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/surrogates/"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: Surrogacy in CT | Become a Surrogate Mother in New Haven, Connecticut\n\nContent: New Haven, Connecticut – Gestational Surrogacy Choose the ConceiveAbilities gestational surrogacy program in New Haven, Hartford, Bridgeport, Fairfield County, Stamford, and other communities throughout Connecticut. ConceiveAbilities’ dedicated team in New Haven, Connecticut connects gestational surrogates with intended parents to help make their dreams come true. Since 1996, our dedicated team of professionals has kept an often complex process as rewarding as possible for every person involved in the family building experience. We invite those interested in surrogacy to learn more about us and to experience the superior support we offer surrogate mothers. We promise to be right there with you through every step of your journey as you help create a new family for a deserving couple. Is surrogacy legal in New Haven, Connecticut?Yes, surrogacy is legal in Connecticut. Parents who reside in Connecticut are allowed to obtain a pre-birth parentage order – regardless of biological relation to the child. Connecticut is generally a very surrogacy-friendly state.How much do surrogate mothers get paid in Connecticut?Surrogate mothers from New Haven, Hartford, Bridgeport, Fairfield County, Stamford, and other communities throughout Connecticut are reimbursed for their efforts in helping deserving couples build their families. As a surrogate mother, you can expect top financial reimbursement from ConceiveAbilities in the form of medical co-pays, maternity clothing, medication, travel (if applicable), costs associated with legal representation, and lost wages (if applicable). We welcome you to learn more about surrogate mother pay before making a decision.Who can become a surrogate mother in New Haven, Connecticut?Interested in joining the ConceiveAbilities Surrogate Sisterhood in New Haven? Before completing an application, we ask that those interested in surrogate motherhood please review our full list of surrogate mother requirements. A few of the initial conditions for eligibility include: Age between 21-43 years old Healthy BMI Have given birth to and be raising at least one child Must provide OB/GYN records and a clearance letter Learn more about becoming a surrogate mother.Testimonials about our surrogacy agency: We are absolutely blessed for having Sandi and her family as a surrogate. She is an excellent woman, an outstanding and strong person and the best mother we know. Her kids are just incredible and her husband, of course, as important to us as Sandi is. LUIS, CONNECTICUT PARENT VIA SURROGACYSurrogacy Laws in Connecticut Connecticut’s surrogacy laws provide a supportive framework for both intended parents and surrogates. The state has specific surrogacy statutes that establish clear guidelines for gestational surrogacy agreements, ensuring legal recognition and protection for all parties involved. With proper legal guidance, intended parents can obtain pre-birth parentage orders to facilitate a smooth and secure process. Connecticut’s legal landscape makes it a welcoming environment for surrogacy journeys. Learn about Connecticut’s Surrogacy LawsGet started with surrogacy in New Haven, CT. Whether you are an intended parent or a surrogate applicant, the process for getting started is easy, secure & confidential. Go ahead and take the first step with ConceiveAbilities. Start Your Parent Journey!Become a Surrogate: Apply Today!Questions? Learn more about ConceiveAbilities. For questions about our agency’s New Haven surrogacy program or more information about becoming a Connecticut surrogate mother, please contact ConceiveAbilities. Visit our contact pageLearn more about surrogacy: Becoming A Surrogate MotherSurrogate Mother ProcessRequirements To Be A Surrogate MotherFrequently Asked Questions about SurrogacySurrogate ApplicationHow Will I Be Compensated As A Surrogate?Using A Surrogate MotherSurrogate MatchingHave You Ever Thought About Becoming A Surrogate?May 6th, 2020Finding a path to parenthood by working with a gestational carrier continues to grow in its appeal. In 2019, an estimated 7,000 births took place by surrogate in the United States, as the market is steadily growing at 15% per year based on data from the Centers for Disease Control. And, in 2017, we know that 4.2% of all In Vitro Fertilization transfers occurred with a gestational carrier, which grew from just over 1% ten years prior.Read MoreWhat to Consider While Being Matched with Intended Parents?September 11th, 2019The decision to become a surrogate means entering into a unique partnership with the intended parents. Think about it: you’re taking on an incredibly powerful responsibility, and it’s often for someone you’ve never met. One of the most important decisions you’ll make during your surrogacy journey is the people you’ll enter into this partnership with. What should you consider when being matched with intended parents? From working with a surrogacy agency to communication and beyond, we’ll explore how to make the very most of your match.Read More",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/about/locations/surrogacy-new-haven-ct/"
  },
